A||N||5||286SE'S||Cavilier Digital Dashboard||||5||
Z||000000||286SE'S||07-31-2000||03:45 PM||||Has anyone done anything with these? I did a search and all I got was "I'm gonna do it" "they look really cool" "the tach is annoying" and "I heard it is a direct swap". I am looking to find out if the dashboard cluster is a self contained unit. I see a connector for the lights "brake, fasten belts, check engine" and a smaller connecter with about 12 wires. That doesn't seem to be enough! Is there a computer or other module sending serial data, located somewhere else in the car? I pulled the digital dash cluster out and need to know if there is anything else I need before the car goes into the crusher. Also if anyone has played with these,; Where can I find the schematic for it? It is made by YAZAKE METER Thanks to anyone who can help!<BR>Terry<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||07-31-2000||10:04 PM||||You actually found one that works? I've worked on them. They generally are not well made. I've only seen a couple that didn't have some kind of problem. I'd seriously consider Cliff's dash over the cavalier units. I just don't trust those old gen 1 dashes. Cliff's may cost more but it will work reliably.<P>My main concern for you would be it works ok now but by the time you get done fighting with it to install it it won't work or will develope the typical intermitant blank display problem that is common. (In this problem, the dash is running and recording odometer data etc but it's vacuum flourecent display will not light up so you can read it.)<P>I imagine it's posible to install it. One critical thing to look at is the sender in the tranny. You need to know if the cavalier unit is A. a pickup coil type or gear driven like Fiero, B. is it 2000 or 4000 pulses per mile. <P>The Fiero dash divides the 4000ppm feed from the sender by 2 then returns that to the ECM. I don't know if the Cav dash can do that. The ECM must have that 2000ppm feed ot the car will not run right. If the dash can't provide the feed you can't just run it off the VSS. It has to go thru shaping and conditioning circuits first or the ECM won't like it.<P>Most of the rest is an issue of working out the wires. (No I don't have any data on cavalier dashes any more.) I think I remember that the tach is programed for 4 or 6 cyl by a jumper/switch someplace in the dash but it's been so long I don't really remember for sure. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||08-01-2000||01:48 AM||||You must be feeling a little better, Ogre.  Glad to hear it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||286SE'S||08-01-2000||10:50 AM||||Thanks for the reply Ogre, The dash I got appears to be one of those dead ones that you mentioned.<BR>It only flashes on when the power is removed so the florescent display DC-DC converter must be bad. The car it came from had a 2.5 engine and I was hoping that it was compatable. I read in my searches that the Chrysler/Dodge Digital dashes are better so I will keep my eye open at the junkyard. Thanks again.<BR>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
